mean — Mean of a variable                                              data · tier 1 · since 0.1.0 · 1.0 · closed form

USAGE
    data.mean(var)

DESCRIPTION
    The arithmetic mean of the non-missing values of `var`. Missing values are skipped with a warning that
    gives their count; a column with no value left, or none at all, gives `NA` with a warning. On a Bool
    column the mean is the share of `true` values, the commonest summary of a treatment indicator. An
    infinite value gives `Inf` or `-Inf`; `Inf` and `-Inf` together give `NA` with a warning.

    Unlike R's `mean`, which needs `na.rm = TRUE`, missing values never make the result `NA` silently: they
    are skipped and counted.

ARGUMENTS
    data        Frame. The frame.
    var         Var. The variable, which must be Bool, Int or Float.

VALUE
    A `Float`.

DETAILS
    The sum is a compensated (Neumaier) sum taken in row order, divided by the count and corrected once by
    the mean of the deviations, so the result is within a rounding of the exact mean whatever the magnitude
    of the values, and the same bits on every target.

WARNINGS AND NOTES
    W0201   missing values skipped, with the count; all missing, or no rows at all, gives NA
    W0204   Inf and -Inf both present, so the result is NA
    Z0101   no such variable in the frame
    Z0102   a variable that differs only in case from the name written
    Z0401   the variable is not Bool, Int or Float

ACCURACY
    Closed form.

REFERENCES
    Neumaier (1974), Rundungsfehleranalyse einiger Verfahren zur Summation endlicher Summen, ZAMM 54, 39–51.
    Higham (2002), Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms, second edition, chapter 4.

SEE ALSO
    sd, summary, describe

EXAMPLES
    > let wages = import("data/wages.csv")
    note: data/wages.csv read as CSV: 3 rows × 3 variables, types guessed; CSV carries no labels, value labels or categories (line 1)
    > wages.mean(wage)
    24.5333
    > wages.mean(hours)
    37.5
    warning: 1 row with NA in hours was skipped (line 3)
